## Template Method for Sorting — Java Implementation

**Pattern mapping:**

| Template Method role | In this design |
|---|---|
| Template method (skeleton) | `SortAlgorithm.sort()` — the entry point every algorithm follows |
| Abstract steps | `compare()` and `swap()` — the only operations on data |
| Concrete products | `BubbleSort`, `SelectionSort` — each fills in its specific skeleton |

Because Bubble Sort and Selection Sort have different loop structures, the abstract class fixes the *contract* (a skeleton built only from `compare()`/`swap()`), and each subclass supplies its specific skeleton. Adding a new algorithm = just another subclass.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

/**
 * Abstract template class. It fixes the contract every sorting
 * algorithm in this family must follow:
 *
 *   sort(array)      – the template method (the algorithm's skeleton)
 *   compare(a, b)    – abstract step used to decide ordering
 *   swap(arr, i, j)  – abstract step used to reorder elements
 *
 * A subclass implements its concrete algorithm as the skeleton of sort(),
 * but may only touch the data through the two abstract steps,
 * so every sort() is composed out of compare() and swap().
 */
public abstract class SortAlgorithm<T extends Comparable<T>> {

    /** Template method: the skeleton of the sorting algorithm. */
    public abstract void sort(T[] array);

    /** Abstract step: compare two elements (sign of a - b). */
    protected abstract int compare(T a, T b);

    /** Abstract step: exchange two positions. */
    protected abstract void swap(T[] array, int i, int j);
}

/** Concrete algorithm: Bubble Sort. */
class BubbleSort<T extends Comparable<T>> extends SortAlgorithm<T> {

    @Override
    public void sort(T[] array) {
        int n = array.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {          // one pass per "bubbled" element
            boolean swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {  // bubble larger neighbor rightward
                if (compare(array[j], array[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    swap(array, j, j + 1);
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            if (!swapped) return;                  // already in order
        }
    }

    @Override
    protected int compare(T a, T b) {
        return a.compareTo(b);
    }

    @Override
    protected void swap(T[] array, int i, int j) {
        T temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}

/** Concrete algorithm: Selection Sort. */
class SelectionSort<T extends Comparable<T>> extends SortAlgorithm<T> {

    @Override
    public void sort(T[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length - 1; i++) {          // fix position i
            int minIndex = i;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < array.length; j++) {      // scan the minimum
                if (compare(array[j], array[minIndex]) < 0) {
                    minIndex = j;
                }
            }
            if (minIndex != i) {
                swap(array, i, minIndex);
            }
        }
    }

    @Override
    protected int compare(T a, T b) {
        return a.compareTo(b);
    }

    @Override
    protected void swap(T[] array, int i, int j) {
        T temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}

/** Test driver. */
public class SortingDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("== Sorting integers ==");
        Integer[] numbers = {45, 23, 12, -9, 7, 38, 0};
        run("BubbleSort",    new BubbleSort<Integer>(),    numbers.clone());
        run("SelectionSort", new SelectionSort<Integer>(), numbers.clone());

        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("== Sorting strings ==");
        String[] words = {"pear", "apple", "kiwi", "banana", "fig"};
        run("BubbleSort",    new BubbleSort<String>(),    words.clone());
        run("SelectionSort", new SelectionSort<String>(), words.clone());
    }

    private static <T> void run(String name, SortAlgorithm<T> algorithm, T[] data) {
        System.out.printf("%-14s before: %s%n", name, Arrays.toString(data));
        algorithm.sort(data);
        System.out.printf("%-14s after:  %s%n", name, Arrays.toString(data));
    }
}
```

